{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Yellow hair.</b> In Torat Kohanim: ״צָהֹב״ — yellow and not greenish, etc.; this excludes all these [other colors] but does not exclude white — and the reasoning is a fortiori: now if yellow, which is not a sign of impurity in nega'im, is a sign of impurity in netakim, then white hair, which is a sign of impurity [in nega'im,] all the more so [in netakim] — therefore Scripture says ״צָהֹב״; thus far. And it is difficult: perhaps ״צָהֹב״ comes for its own sake — to teach that it defiles in a netek even though it does not defile in other nega'im — and in any case the white defiles by means of the a fortiori argument mentioned. And furthermore, since the Tanna did not make an a fortiori argument — ״and what yellow,״ etc. — until he took in hand that the yellow defiles in a netek, it follows that even though Scripture said ״yellow״ regarding a netek, it made an a fortiori argument for the white. If so, by what did he negate it [the a fortiori reasoning] by saying ״תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר״?" "<b>And it appears</b> that the Tanna's derashah is from the fact that Scripture said ״צָהֹב וְטִמֵּא אֹתוֹ״ — and did not say ״וְטִמְּאוֹ״ as it said in two or three places — and said ״אֹתוֹ״ to exclude the white, in this manner: ״אֹתוֹ״ means the yellow defiles, and it is as though the Tanna said: תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר ״צָהֹב וְטִמֵּא אֹתוֹ״. And likewise you will find that the Tanna in Torat Kohanim derived all the instances of ״אֹתוֹ״ mentioned in the preceding parashot from the fact that Scripture did not say ״וְטִמְּאוֹ״. And the reason the word ״אֹתוֹ״ did not appear in the Tanna's own words is because he still wished to derive the word ״דָּק״ that preceded it, and he did not take notice of the omission, for it is not the way [of the Tanna] to turn right and left but to rely on the source. And you will find that afterward the Tanna derived an exclusion from ״וְטִמֵּא אֹתוֹ נֶתֶק הוּא״, and derived nothing from it except the exclusion of ״הוּא״ and not the exclusion of ״אֹתוֹ״, since he had already derived it in our manner." "<b>And if</b> you say: if so, let us reason an a fortiori argument to defile yellow in other nega'im along these lines — and what if white, which does not defile in a netek, does defile in other nega'im, then yellow, which defiles in a netek, all the more so should it defile in other nega'im. But perhaps, since it was necessary to exclude white in a netek, this tells us that He was concerned not to reason by a fortiori, and it is inconceivable that an a fortiori argument would be made to the point of justifying saying that yellow does not defile in other nega'im. From this there is no place to reason the reverse, since Scripture has testified that yellow does not defile in other nega'im — from the fact that it was necessary to exclude white in a netek as mentioned — and understand. And see in parashat Metzora (Leviticus 14:7) what we discussed regarding the baraita of ״seven times said regarding the metzora.״" "And the meaning of ״yellow״ — they said in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: ״צָהֹב״ etc. — and what is it like? Like the appearance of gold. And Rambam (Negaim 6:1) explained that it is an appearance mixed of reddishness and greenishness; thus far. And I saw that R. Avraham ibn Ezra, of blessed memory, explained it, and this is his language: in the Ishmaelite tongue it is close to the nature of white; thus far. It appears that he would think this is what Scripture intended, and we are of the exile of Ishmael, and that hue which they call in the Ishmaelite tongue is indeed somewhat white — and according to this it is the opposite of the explanation of the Tannaim. And with apologies for his honor: if we were to disagree with our Sages of blessed memory and explain the words of the Torah in the Ishmaelite tongue, we would be making a Torah of falsehood. Is this interpretation, then, ugly — which our Sages of blessed memory explained as ״the appearance of gold״? And in explanations such as these the Master gave a hand to the sages of the generations to belittle his honor, and any explanation of this kind — the words should be sunk and never said."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {3 "The phrase וְכִי כְעוּרָה זוֹ is read hyper-literally; the logical connector וְכִי is rendered as a rhetorical question, and כְעוּרָה as 'ugly,' preserving the Or HaChaim's sharp objection to Ibn Ezra's explanation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:21.724737745Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}